---
# Copy to copyparty-accounts.secret.yaml and fill in. The rendered Secret
# is applied with kubectl directly, not committed (gitignored via
# the repo-wide **/*secret*.yaml rule).
#
# Generate a strong password with:  openssl rand -base64 24
#
# The `noot` account has `A` (admin) on both /files and /media -- read,
# write, move, delete, get, dots, and user-admin on each (TASK-1083
# intentionally widened both from the original rwmd/r split; this is a
# single-owner instance, and /media is additionally RO at the NFS mount
# level regardless of the ACL). See copyparty-deployment.yaml's volume-spec
# comment for the full rationale.
#
# PASSWORD is plaintext here. copyparty-deployment.yaml (IMPR-1009) reads
# USER/PASSWORD from this Secret and writes them into a generated `-c`
# config file at container start instead of putting them on the command
# line, so the value no longer appears in `ps`/argv -- but it is still
# plaintext at rest in this Secret and in the container's /cfg config file.
#
# --ah-alg argon2 (hashed-password mode) is NOT enabled. copyparty's own
# --help-pwhash is explicit that once --ah-alg is non-default, EVERY account
# password must already be hashed (prefixed `+`) or copyparty hashes it,
# prints the hash, and exits without serving -- i.e. flipping --ah-alg on
# while this Secret holds a plaintext PASSWORD would crash-loop the
# deployment on its next restart, not silently keep working. To enable it
# later: generate a hash from the real password with
# `python3 -m copyparty --ah-alg argon2 --ah-gen '<password>'` (or
# `--ah-cli` to avoid the plaintext ever touching a shell history), replace
# PASSWORD here with the resulting `+`-prefixed hash, apply the Secret, THEN
# add `--ah-alg argon2` to the deployment args in the same change -- and
# confirm login still works before considering it done.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: copyparty-accounts
  namespace: copyparty
type: Opaque
stringData:
  USER: "noot"
  PASSWORD: "CHANGE_ME_LONG_RANDOM_STRING"
